# Proposed authorship for counted facts and worked examples

For a question that explicitly requests two capabilities, a unique branch-bound source sentence can support two separately required actions. Both must retain their common subject and qualifier. For a quantity requested from an example, keep the verified conditions and result in the same evidence-bound claim. Other question shapes continue through the general workflow. Neither path computes figures or reads answer keys.

The offline projection proved that verified fields can produce a cited candidate for each consumed case. The smart-meter candidate preserves “some” and both actions. The bulb candidate preserves 40 watts and five hours with 200 Wh. The prototype also rejected omitted or substituted fields in 12 checks using unrelated refrigerated-locker and sprinkler facts. These are application-projected candidates. The prior technical, source and owner decisions remain on their original artifacts.

Two authorship options remain:

- Use required raw source fields with application rendering, as in the verified category-definition path. This guarantees both verified phrases when the private response passes exact matching and keeps the conditions attached to their result. Identify that rendering separately in diagnostics. This is the recommended offline option.
- Keep model-written prose with required, independently validated statements. This allows more fluent wording but needs an offline validation contract that recognizes a supported paraphrase and rejects incomplete or negated relationships.

Both choices retain the existing final safety check, citation binding, stored review and exact-revision approval rules, existing public schema and one composer call. Neither choice creates a model-run grant. Keep the ten unattempted cases reserved. No inference, safety or storage test ran in this prototype.
